    "user": "Please continue. \nWhat is the intuition, rather than the formula?",
    "response": "The Jacobian matrix captures how a function transforms space locally. Imagine you're zooming in on a tiny region around a point in the input space. The Jacobian tells you how that tiny region is stretched, rotated, or sheared when the function maps it to the output space. Each column of the Jacobian corresponds to how the function changes along one input axis, while the rows show how each output component changes with respect to those inputs. Essentially, it's a way to quantify the local \"distortion\" caused by the function.",
